# __REPO__ issues

Everything is native GitHub Issues, driven with `gh`. Run commands with `-R __REPO__` (shown as `-R $R` below, with `R=__REPO__`).

## Model

| Linear | Here |
|---|---|
| Triage | open, labeled `triage` (issue forms add it) |
| Backlog | open, not assigned |
| In Progress | open, assigned |
| In Review | open, and an open PR closes it (`Fixes #N`) |
| Done | closed as completed |
| Canceled | closed as not planned |
| Duplicate | closed as duplicate |
| Type | label: `bug`, `feature`, `chore` |
| Priority | label: `urgent`, `high`, `medium`, `low`. At most one. No label means no priority |
| Project | parent issue labeled `project`, with native sub-issues. Progress = sub-issues done |
| Blocked by | native issue dependencies |

There are no cycles and no estimates. Milestones exist if the user wants them, but nothing here manages them.

When you change an issue's priority, remove the old priority label in the same command, for example `gh issue edit N -R $R --remove-label high --add-label urgent`.

## Triage

```
gh issue list -R $R --label triage --json number,title,labels,createdAt,url \
  --jq 'sort_by(.createdAt)[] | "#\(.number)\t\(.title)\t\([.labels[].name] | join(","))"'
```

For each issue, read it (`gh issue view N -R $R`), propose a type and priority, and ask the user. Go one at a time unless the user wants a batch. Then:

- Accept: `gh issue edit N -R $R --remove-label triage --add-label high` (plus the type label if it is missing).
- Decline: `gh issue close N -R $R --reason "not planned" --comment "Declined: REASON"`.
- Duplicate: `gh issue close N -R $R --duplicate-of M`.

End with how many you accepted, declined, and closed as duplicate.

## Create

Ask only for what is missing. The type comes from context. Issues filed here skip triage, because the user is filing them on purpose.

```
gh issue create -R $R --title "..." --body "..." --label feature --label high \
  [--parent P] [--blocked-by 3,5] [--assignee @me]
```

When you file a batch of related issues, create the blockers first so you can reference them. Report the URLs.

## Next

```
gh issue list -R $R --state open --limit 500 \
  --json number,title,labels,assignees,blockedBy,createdAt \
  --jq '
    def prio: ([.labels[].name] as $l | ["urgent","high","medium","low"] | map(. as $p | $l | index($p)) | to_entries | map(select(.value != null)) | .[0].key // 4);
    def open_blockers: [.blockedBy.nodes[]? | select(.state == "OPEN") | "#\(.number)"];
    [.[] | select([.labels[].name] | (index("triage") or index("project")) | not)]
    | map(. + {p: prio, b: open_blockers})
    | (map(select(.assignees | length > 0)) | "## Now", (.[] | "#\(.number)\t\(.title)\t@\(.assignees[0].login)")),
      (map(select((.assignees | length == 0) and (.b | length == 0))) | sort_by(.p, .createdAt) | .[:5] | "## Next", (.[] | "#\(.number)\t\(.title)\t\(["urgent","high","medium","low","-"][.p])")),
      (map(select((.assignees | length == 0) and (.b | length > 0))) | sort_by(.p, .createdAt) | "## Blocked", (.[] | "#\(.number)\t\(.title)\twaits on \(.b | join(", "))"))'
```

To mark which Now issues are in review, check `gh pr list -R $R --json number,closingIssuesReferences --jq '.[] | "PR #\(.number) closes \([.closingIssuesReferences[].number] | map("#\(.)") | join(", "))"'`.

Suggest the top Next issue. If a blocked issue ranks higher, say what it waits on, and suggest its blockers first.

## Start and finish

- Start: `gh issue edit N -R $R --add-assignee @me`. Suggest a branch named `N-short-slug` and a PR body with `Fixes #N`, so merging closes the issue.
- Done without a PR: `gh issue close N -R $R --reason completed`.
- Put back in the backlog: `gh issue edit N -R $R --remove-assignee @me`.

## Block and unblock

- `gh issue edit N -R $R --add-blocked-by 3,5`
- `gh issue edit N -R $R --remove-blocked-by 3`

A closed blocker no longer blocks.

## Projects

- Create: `gh issue create -R $R --title "..." --body "Goal and scope" --label project --assignee LEAD`.
- Add work: `gh issue edit P -R $R --add-sub-issue 12,13`, or create the child with `--parent P`.
- Each sub-issue is its own spec, built whole by one builder: the MVP, or a branch (a feature added on top of the MVP). Never split one spec into sub-issues by layer (backend, frontend, tests) or by step: what one builder learns mid-build never reaches the others. Keep progress on one spec as a checklist in its body.
- Branches wait for the MVP: mark each branch `--add-blocked-by <MVP>`. A branch can start in parallel with another only if they don't touch the same files, data or decisions; otherwise block one on the other. Until the MVP merges, a branch's body is just its one-line goal. Write its full spec against the merged code before starting it.
- Progress:

```
gh issue list -R $R --state open --label project --json number,title,assignees,subIssuesSummary \
  --jq '.[] | "#\(.number)\t\(.title)\t\(.subIssuesSummary.completed)/\(.subIssuesSummary.total) done\t@\(.assignees[0].login // "-")"'
```

Close a project issue (`--reason completed`) when its work is done.

## Stale

Open issues with no activity for 60 days:

```
gh issue list -R $R --state open --search "updated:<$(date -v-60d +%F 2>/dev/null || date -d '60 days ago' +%F)" \
  --json number,title,updatedAt --jq '.[] | "#\(.number)\t\(.title)\tlast touched \(.updatedAt[:10])"'
```

For each one, suggest keep, reprioritize, or decline. Ask before you change anything.

## Log

What got done in the last N days (default 7):

```
gh issue list -R $R --state closed --limit 200 --search "closed:>=$(date -v-7d +%F 2>/dev/null || date -d '7 days ago' +%F) reason:completed" \
  --json number,title,labels,closedAt --jq 'sort_by(.closedAt)[] | "\(.closedAt[:10])\t#\(.number)\t\(.title)"'
```

Summarize it in a few lines grouped by type or project. It is a log, not a status report: no deadlines or at-risk language.

## Rules

- Ask before you close, decline, or bulk-edit issues.
- Keep at most one priority label on an issue.
- Re-run the setup (`github-issues-setup`) to repair labels or issue forms. It overwrites this file.
